---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { dwarfPlanets } from "../../data/solar-system";
const dwarfs = [
  { name:"Ceres", region:"Main asteroid belt", fact:"The only dwarf planet in the inner Solar System. NASA's Dawn spacecraft found bright deposits, evidence of past and possibly present brine-related activity, and a complex icy-rocky history." },
  { name:"Pluto", region:"Kuiper Belt", fact:"A complex icy world with mountains, glaciers, a thin nitrogen-rich atmosphere and five known moons. New Horizons revealed a world far more geologically diverse than expected." },
  { name:"Haumea", region:"Trans-Neptunian region", fact:"A rapidly rotating, elongated dwarf planet with a ring and two known moons. Its shape reflects its unusual spin and internal structure." },
  { name:"Makemake", region:"Kuiper Belt", fact:"A bright, methane-rich distant world with a small moon discovered through observations. Its surface chemistry and seasonal atmosphere are active research topics." },
  { name:"Eris", region:"Scattered-disc region", fact:"A massive, distant dwarf planet with a known moon, Dysnomia. Its discovery helped prompt the debate that led to the 2006 planet definition." }
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Dwarf planets" description="Understand the IAU definition of dwarf planets and explore Ceres, Pluto, Haumea, Makemake and Eris.">
  <div class="container breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/explore/">Explore</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Dwarf planets</span></div>
  <header class="page-intro container"><div class="eyebrow">DWARF PLANETS</div><h1>Five recognised dwarf planets.</h1><p class="lede">Dwarf planets are round worlds that orbit the Sun but have not cleared the neighbourhood around their orbits.</p></header>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="definition-panel card"><div><span class="eyebrow">The 2006 IAU definition</span><h2>What makes a dwarf planet?</h2><p>A dwarf planet orbits the Sun, has enough self-gravity to become nearly round, has not cleared its orbital neighbourhood, and is not a satellite.</p></div><div class="criteria-list"><div><span>01</span><strong>Orbits the Sun</strong></div><div><span>02</span><strong>Nearly round from self-gravity</strong></div><div><span>03</span><strong>Has not cleared its orbital zone</strong></div><div><span>04</span><strong>Is not a moon</strong></div></div></div>
    <div class="section-head dwarf-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">Five widely recognised dwarf planets</div><h2>The five recognised dwarf planets.</h2></div><p>These five are commonly recognised as dwarf planets in our Solar System. Many additional trans-Neptunian objects are candidates, but their classification is not settled.</p></div>
    <div class="dwarf-grid">{dwarfs.map((item,i)=><article class="card dwarf-card"><span class="dwarf-symbol">{["◉","●","⬭","◌","✧"][i]}</span><span class="eyebrow">{item.region}</span><h3>{item.name}</h3><p>{item.fact}</p></article>)}</div>

    <div class="dwarf-physical-reference">
      <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">MEASURED PHYSICAL PROPERTIES</span><h2>How small are they?</h2></div><p>These values use JPL's mean radius, bulk density, equatorial surface gravity and escape velocity. The mass and dimensions of distant bodies can carry larger uncertainties.</p></div>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table class="dwarf-physical-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Mass (kg)</th><th>Mean radius</th><th>Density</th><th>Equatorial gravity</th><th>Escape velocity</th><th>Rotation</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{dwarfPlanets.map((d) => <tr><th>{d.name}</th><td>{d.massKg.toExponential(3)} kg</td><td>{d.meanRadiusKm.toLocaleString("en-US")} km</td><td>{d.densityGcm3} g/cm³</td><td>{d.equatorialGravityMS2} m/s²</td><td>{d.escapeVelocityKmS} km/s</td><td>{d.siderealRotationDays} days</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="dwarf-data-note">The JPL table reports an equivalent-volume mean radius. Haumea is markedly elongated; its mean radius is not the length of its longest axis. Values are rounded for display. <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/phys_par.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">JPL source table ↗</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="dwarf-real-images">
      <figure><img src="https://d2pn8kiwq2w21t.cloudfront.net/images/jpegPIA22485.width-1024.jpg" alt="NASA Dawn spacecraft view of Ceres's Occator Crater and bright salt deposits." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Ceres · NASA Dawn · Occator Crater and bright deposits</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia19/pia19952/PIA19952.jpg?w=1536&h=1536&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint" alt="Enhanced-colour New Horizons image of Pluto showing its varied surface." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Pluto · NASA New Horizons · Enhanced colour, not natural colour</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <p class="dwarf-image-note">Ceres and Pluto have been visited by spacecraft, so their photographs resolve surface features. Haumea, Makemake and Eris have not been visited; telescope observations generally do not resolve their surfaces into detailed photographs. Their sizes, shapes and compositions are inferred from other measurements.</p>
    <div class="prose dwarf-prose">
      <h2>Why did Pluto's classification change?</h2>
      <p>As astronomers discovered more objects in the outer Solar System, it became clear that Pluto belongs to a wider population of icy bodies beyond Neptune. In 2006, the International Astronomical Union adopted a formal definition for planets in the Solar System. Under that definition, Pluto is a dwarf planet because it has not cleared its orbital neighbourhood.</p>
      <p>This is a classification, not a demotion of scientific importance. Pluto is still a complex world with active-looking geology, a layered atmosphere, a system of moons and a remarkable exploration history.</p>
      <h2>What about the other candidates?</h2>
      <p>Objects such as Gonggong, Quaoar, Sedna, Orcus and many others are sometimes discussed as possible dwarf planets. Whether an object is round enough, and therefore in hydrostatic equilibrium, can be difficult to establish at great distances. ORRERYA keeps candidates separate from the five commonly recognised dwarf planets rather than presenting an uncertain list as settled fact.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>The key distinction is orbital neighbourhood.</strong><p>Being round is not enough to be classified as a planet under the IAU definition. A planet must also gravitationally dominate and clear its orbital zone. Dwarf planets have not done so.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="source-panel"><div><span class="eyebrow">Classification sources</span><h3>How the IAU defines a planet</h3><p>Use the original resolution and NASA's current overview for the classification context.</p></div><ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://www.iau.org/static/resolutions/Resolution_GA26-5-6.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">International Astronomical Union — Resolution B5: Definition of a Planet in the Solar System (2006) ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Dwarf Planets ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/phys_par.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA/JPL — Planetary Physical Parameters ↗</a></li></ul></div>
  </section>
  <style>.definition-panel{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr .9fr;gap:30px;padding:30px}.definition-panel h2{font-size:2rem;margin:10px 0}.definition-panel p{line-height:1.8;max-width:600px}.criteria-list{display:grid;gap:7px}.criteria-list div{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;padding:11px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:5px;background:rgba(8,13,24,.45)}.criteria-list span{color:var(--gold);font-size:.72rem}.criteria-list strong{font-size:.82rem}.dwarf-head{margin-top:75px}.dwarf-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(5,1fr);gap:12px}.dwarf-card{padding:19px}.dwarf-symbol{display:block;font-size:2.1rem;color:var(--gold);margin-bottom:22px}.dwarf-card h3{font:600 1.25rem "Space Grotesk",sans-serif;margin:9px 0}.dwarf-card p{font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0}.dwarf-prose{margin-top:55px}.source-panel{margin-top:40px}@media(max-width:980px){.dwarf-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr)}}@media(max-width:720px){.definition-panel{grid-template-columns:1fr}.dwarf-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}}@media(max-width:460px){.dwarf-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}</style>

  <style>
    .dwarf-physical-reference{margin:48px 0}
    .dwarf-physical-table{width:100%;min-width:940px;border-collapse:collapse;background:#fff}
    .dwarf-physical-table th,.dwarf-physical-table td{padding:12px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #e1e8ed;font-size:.76rem;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
    .dwarf-physical-table thead th{background:#edf2f5;color:#51677b;font-size:.68rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.06em}
    .dwarf-physical-table tbody th{color:#173b55}.dwarf-physical-table tbody td{color:#52687b}
    .dwarf-data-note,.dwarf-image-note{font-size:.74rem;line-height:1.7;color:#62798b;margin:12px 0}.dwarf-data-note a{color:#1b5d82}
    .dwarf-real-images{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:14px;margin:22px 0}
    .dwarf-real-images figure{margin:0;border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden;background:#0b1928}
    .dwarf-real-images img{width:100%;height:270px;object-fit:contain;display:block}
    .dwarf-real-images figcaption{padding:10px 12px;color:#c8d3dc;font-size:.7rem}
    @media(max-width:600px){.dwarf-real-images{grid-template-columns:1fr}.dwarf-real-images img{height:230px}}
  </style>
</SiteLayout>
